README

This directory contains the Circle port of JSD (JPL Servo Drive), which allows
to command and control Elmo servo drives over EtherCAT from a bare metal
application. JSD is developed by the Jet Propulsion Laboratory, California
Institute of Technology, and is not part of Circle:

	https://github.com/nasa-jpl/jsd

JSD is distributed under a 3-clause BSD license. Please see the file LICENSE in
the JSD sources for the full license information.

This port supports the Elmo drives only:

	* Elmo Gold drives (jsd_egd)
	* Elmo Platinum drives in nominal (DS-402) mode (jsd_epd_nominal)

The other device drivers, which come with JSD (Beckhoff EL terminals, ATI
force/torque sensors, JPL JED boards, Micro-Epsilon ILD1900 and the Platinum
drives in SIL mode), are not built, but can be added (see below).


GET THE JSD SOURCES

Change to this directory (addon/jsd) and enter:

	git clone https://github.com/nasa-jpl/jsd.git

This port has been developed against JSD 3.1.8 (branch "master"). The build
system of JSD (CMake) is not used. Only the include directory, which the CMake
build normally generates, is created as a symbolic link by the Makefile.


BUILD

The Circle libraries and the SOEM library in addon/soem have to be built first
(see addon/soem/README). Then enter here:

	make

This creates the library libjsd.a. If the JSD sources have been cloned to
another directory, the variable JSDHOME can be set:

	make JSDHOME=/path/to/jsd

The internal debug messages of JSD are disabled by default and can be enabled
with:

	make JSD_DEBUG=1

A demonstration of this library is in sample/48-elmoweb.


HOW THE PORT WORKS

JSD is written against SOEM 1.4 (with the build option EC_VER2, which selects
the context API) and against a POSIX system with threads, while Circle comes
with a port of SOEM 2.0 and has its own cooperative scheduler. The two versions
of SOEM are not source compatible: SOEM 1.4 keeps the slave list and all
internal buffers behind pointers in ecx_contextt, which the application has to
allocate, while SOEM 2.0 embeds them in the context.

Fortunately the SOEM dependency of JSD is concentrated in two files: only
src/jsd.c and src/jsd_sdo.c call SOEM functions. All device drivers, including
the Elmo drivers, access the SOEM context through its data members only
(slavelist[n].inputs, slavelist[n].outputs, ...), which work the same in both
versions. That's why the device drivers are used unmodified here and only the
bus layer has been rewritten:

  Original		Replacement			Reason
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  src/jsd.c		circle/jsd_circle.cpp		SOEM 2.0, Circle tasks
  src/jsd_sdo.c		circle/jsd_sdo_circle.cpp	SOEM 2.0, Circle tasks
  src/jsd_print.h	include/jsd/jsd_print.h		CLogger instead of stdio
  src/jsd_time.h	include/jsd/jsd_time.h		CTimer instead of POSIX
  src/jsd_timer.c	(dropped)			Circle owns the loop
  <pthread.h>		compat/pthread.h		mutexes on CMutex
  <stdlib.h>		compat/stdlib.h			see the file
  <unistd.h>		compat/unistd.h			see the file
  ethercat.h		compat/ethercat.h		maps SOEM 1.4 to 2.0

These files are used unmodified from the JSD sources:

	jsd_egd.c, jsd_epd_nominal.c, jsd_epd_common.c, jsd_elmo_common.c,
	jsd_common_device_types.c, jsd_error_cirq.c and all headers

The compatibility header compat/ethercat.h maps the name of the slave
configuration hook (PO2SOconfigx in SOEM 1.4, PO2SOconfig in SOEM 2.0) and
includes soemcpp.h in C++ files, which resolves the different definitions of
the type "boolean" in SOEM and Circle.

Some functions, which JSD expects from the C library, are provided by the port,
because Circle does not have them: bsearch(3) and strdup(3) in
circle/jsd_support.cpp, and snprintf(3) in the Circle port of SOEM.


DIFFERENCES IN BEHAVIOR

* The asynchronous SDO requests are serviced by a Circle task ("jsdsdo"), not
  by a POSIX thread. The slaves with a mailbox are registered at the cyclic
  mailbox handler of SOEM 2.0, which is pumped from jsd_read() once per process
  data cycle. This way an SDO transfer, which can take up to 1.4 seconds, does
  not stall the process data cycle of the application.
* The transition to the OPERATIONAL state exchanges process data continuously,
  while it waits for the state change. The original implementation waits for up
  to EC_TIMEOUTSTATE without sending process data, which some drives do not
  tolerate.
* jsd_ec_state_to_string() returns a static string for unknown states. The
  original implementation returns strdup(), which the caller cannot free
  consistently.
* jsd_alloc() does not allocate the SOEM context members, because SOEM 2.0
  embeds them.

The API of JSD is unchanged otherwise: an application calls jsd_alloc(),
jsd_set_slave_config() for each drive, jsd_init(), and then cyclically
jsd_read(), jsd_<driver>_read(), the command functions, jsd_<driver>_process()
and jsd_write().


ADDING MORE DEVICE DRIVERS

To use another device driver of JSD, add its source file to JSDSRCS in the
Makefile and add the driver type to jsd_init_single_device() and
jsd_driver_is_compatible_with_product_code() in circle/jsd_circle.cpp. Devices,
which use their own threads or the C library, may need more work.


REQUIREMENTS AND LIMITATIONS

* The Circle scheduler (lib/sched) and the SOEM library (addon/soem) are
  required. jsd_init() fails without the scheduler.
* The scheduler of Circle is cooperative and non-preemptive. The application
  has to call the cyclic functions at a constant rate, which has to match the
  configured loop_period_ms of the drives, and no other task may block for
  longer than the cycle time. Cycle times below one millisecond are not
  recommended.
* The size of the process data image is JSD_IOMAP_BYTES (4096) bytes, as in the
  original JSD.
* Line redundancy is not supported by the Circle port of SOEM.
* This port has not been tested against real Elmo hardware.
